1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,640
It stands tall as a grain, becomes beer with yeast,

2
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:10,340
grows powerful in a still, and old in a barrel,

3
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:13,240
only to end up tasting like everything from the sea

4
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:16,140
to grass to caramel.

5
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:20,060
We'll toast St. Patrick in Dublin and have a dram in Denver.

6
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:23,260
And we'll find out what makes scotches single, Tennessee

7
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:26,300
whiskeys straight, and Canadians great mixers.

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,160
Whether you call it moonshine, bourbon,

9
00:00:28,161 --> 00:00:30,200
bourbon, or even the water of life.

10
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:32,000
It's a real liquid asset.

11
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:36,760
We have bottles in here that will fetch $500 a glass.

12
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:40,500
Now, the untold story of whiskey on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:57,520
A fire rages at the Jack Daniel Distillery

14
00:00:57,521 --> 00:00:59,480
in Lynchburg, Tennessee.

15
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:01,220
But rather than dampen it, workers

16
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:02,920
stoke it with an unusual fuel.

17
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:06,760
We're using 140-proof whiskey to start the fire

18
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:10,160
because if we use gasoline or diesel or anything like that,

19
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:12,360
it'll contaminate the charcoal.

20
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:14,540
Each one of these stacks of wood is called a rick.

21
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:18,880
It takes 16 ricks to fill a vat of charcoal.

22
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:21,280
To remove impurities from their whiskey,

23
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:23,780
distillers at Jack Daniels filter every drop of it

24
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:25,680
through vats of sugar maple charcoal

25
00:01:25,681 --> 00:01:30,120
they manufacture on site. This process is the one thing

26
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:32,780
that we do, manufacturing Jack Daniels, that distinguishes us

27
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:34,960
from a bourbon. This lone flavor

28
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:37,820
modification makes Jack Daniels a Tennessee straight

29
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:42,160
whiskey and not a bourbon. Bourbon made with a majority

30
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:46,560
of corn can't have any flavor modification.

31
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:50,440
It's just distillate and water, aged in oak barrels.

32
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:53,040
The whiskey category is ripe with these kinds of subtle

33
00:01:53,041 --> 00:01:56,180
distinctions. There are Tennessee whiskeys,

34
00:01:56,280 --> 00:02:00,340
bourbons, Irish whiskeys, Canadian whiskeys, scotches,

35
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:04,780
ryes, even a Colorado whiskey. Though distinct, they're all

36
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:07,180
whiskeys because they have one thing in common.

37
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:09,800
Well, the main thing that differentiates whiskey from the

38
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:12,400
other spirits in the world is the fact that whiskeys are made

39
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:15,900
from grain. A major grain producer,

40
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,800
the United States is also the world's number two producer of

41
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:22,340
whiskey behind Scotland. Canada, Japan, and Ireland

42
00:02:22,341 --> 00:02:26,740
follow. Whiskey is the world's second most popular distilled

43
00:02:26,741 --> 00:02:30,740
spirit after vodka, and the US consumes more of it than anyone

44
00:02:30,741 --> 00:02:34,180
else. Jack Daniels holds the title of the world's number one

45
00:02:34,181 --> 00:02:40,100
brand of whiskey, selling almost 24 million gallons per year.

46
00:02:40,101 --> 00:02:43,500
Most American whiskeys, like Jack Daniels, fall into a subgroup

47
00:02:43,501 --> 00:02:47,740
called straight whiskey. To be designated a straight whiskey,

48
00:02:47,741 --> 00:02:51,140
a manufacturer must follow tight governmental guidelines.

49
00:02:51,141 --> 00:02:52,800
When you're making a straight whiskey,

50
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:56,980
it can only be distilled to no more than 160 proof.

51
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:01,220
And it can't go into a barrel no more than 125 proof.

52
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:05,160
And it has to be aged in new charred oak barrels

53
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:07,120
for a minimum of two years.

54
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:09,900
And on top of that, each style of straight whiskey

55
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,500
has to have a major grain component.

56
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:15,300
What makes one whiskey different from another

57
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:18,240
is the type of grains that are used, the way that they're

58
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:20,340
distilled, and how they're aged.

59
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:24,040
These differences lead to distinct flavor profiles,

60
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:25,980
like that of Jack Daniels.

61
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:28,280
Ethan Kelly is a spirit sommelier,

62
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:30,180
a trained expert in the hard stuff.

63
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:32,180
At the Brandy Library in New York City,

64
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:34,920
he oversees one of the country's most extensive whiskey

65
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:36,460
collections.

66
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:38,800
Ethan gives Jack a taste test.

67
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:43,660
When you notice this one, the wood does pop right out.

68
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:46,660
And it is clear that you're in a forest.

69
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:49,860
You do get some very earthy qualities to it,

70
00:03:49,861 --> 00:03:52,260
almost farmhouse qualities.

71
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:58,360
It's fatty, and it's earthy, and it's sweet.

72
00:03:58,460 --> 00:04:02,800
That farmhouse quality comes from, well, the farm.

73
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:06,820
Jack Daniels starts with a recipe, also known as a grain

74
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:13,820
bill, of 80% corn, 12% barley, and 8% rye.

75
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:18,560
The distillery, located on this site since the 1860s, has its

76
00:04:18,561 --> 00:04:23,200
water source that also influences flavor.

77
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:25,800
Jack Daniel purchased the whiskey-making operation

78
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:27,900
from a Lutheran minister in his early teens,

79
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,400
but he located the manufacturing operation here

80
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:33,600
because of an abundant limestone water.

81
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:36,720
It's an iron-free water source that flows out of the cave

82
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:40,720
at a constant 56 degrees year-round.

83
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:43,820
This lack of iron affects the fermentation process.

84
00:04:43,821 --> 00:04:46,620
Yeast likes to have all of the metals present,

85
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:48,060
a balanced diet.

86
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:51,760
So what we've done is by supplying it with iron-free water,

87
00:04:51,860 --> 00:04:54,760
we tend to shift the fermentation just a little bit,

88
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:58,260
and that shift is part of what makes Jack Daniels.

89
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:00,200
The grain and water are pumped into what's

90
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:04,640
known as the mash cooker and cooked into a runny porridge.

91
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:06,880
Temperatures of up to 210 degrees

92
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:08,780
break down the walls of the grain,

93
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:11,740
allowing water to soak the starches.

94
00:05:11,741 --> 00:05:14,940
Enzymes in the grain can then convert starches into sugars,

95
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:17,980
readying them for fermentation.

96
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:21,420
This mash is then transferred into 40,000-gallon fermenting

97
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:25,480
vats, where for the next three to five days, yeast goes to work

98
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:28,160
making alcohol.

99
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:30,220
All the agitation that you see here

100
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:33,260
is strictly from the yeast acting on the fermentable

101
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:34,260
sugars.

102
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:36,740
And they start converting fermentable sugars

103
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:38,860
to CO2 and ethanol.

104
00:05:38,861 --> 00:05:41,500
So when I see a bubble of CO2 in here,

105
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:45,000
I've also got a bubble of ethanol that I've made.

106
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:47,260
Ethanol, the alcohol that the yeast converts

107
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:50,940
from the grain's sugars, reaches about 10% concentration

108
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:53,340
by the end of fermentation.

109
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:57,840
This alcoholic mash is now known as distiller's beer.

110
00:05:57,940 --> 00:05:59,680
The distiller's beer is a little different

111
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:03,280
than normal beer in that we ferment the whole grain.

112
00:06:03,380 --> 00:06:07,260
What we're doing here is we're pumping the distiller's beer

113
00:06:07,261 --> 00:06:10,700
beer to the top of the beer's still at about 250 gallons

114
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,100
a minute.

115
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:14,560
We add the steam in the bottom of the still.

116
00:06:14,660 --> 00:06:17,900
When we do that, then the steam goes up through the column.

117
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,780
There's a series of what we call stripping trays in here.

118
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:25,280
Those stripping trays have several hundred holes in them.

119
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:27,520
The column of steam collides with the distiller's

120
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:30,980
beer, vaporizing the more volatile alcohol which passes

121
00:06:31,080 --> 00:06:33,160
out the top of the still.

122
00:06:33,260 --> 00:06:36,220
The perforated trays allow the grain husks and water that

123
00:06:36,221 --> 00:06:39,060
of alcohol to flow out of the bottom of the still.

124
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:44,160
The final distillate is a clear grain alcohol.

125
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:46,160
Now, this is 140 proof, and there's

126
00:06:46,260 --> 00:06:48,860
about 40 gallons a minute coming out of here.

127
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:54,880
I've heard this called white dog, white mule, white lightning.

128
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:56,580
It's just new whiskey.

129
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:01,780
Every drop of this new 140 proof whiskey

130
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:06,220
will be filtered through one of 72 charcoal mellowing bats.

131
00:07:06,221 --> 00:07:09,420
producing as much as 80,000 gallons of filtered whiskey

132
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:11,920
per day.

133
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:14,860
It takes the whiskey approximately one day

134
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:18,260
to work its way down towards the bottom of the charcoal.

135
00:07:18,360 --> 00:07:22,740
This charcoal has very strong absorptive capabilities

136
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:27,340
to bind some of the impurities that come off in the new whiskey

137
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:28,340
through distillation.

138
00:07:28,341 --> 00:07:29,240
distillation.

139
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:38,180
Workers then pump the whiskey into new charred white oak

140
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:38,980
barrels.

141
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:47,400
Each barrel weighs in at 500 pounds

142
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:51,300
and must be loaded into the maturation warehouse by hand.

143
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:55,900
Usually a good entry day is about 1,000 barrels.

144
00:07:55,901 --> 00:07:58,880
It depends on, a lot of it depends on how the house is rolling.

145
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:03,900
The barrels won't roll easily if the warehouse has settled at a tilt.

146
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:08,100
Workers take care to keep the barrel load in the warehouse even,

147
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:12,160
since the pressures of more than 10 million pounds of whiskey can warp the structure.

148
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:17,120
We have 77 maturation warehouses where we store,

149
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:19,820
currently have in storage about 1.7 million barrels.

150
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:24,920
During maturation, the whiskey commingles with a deliberately charred surface

151
00:08:24,921 --> 00:08:30,420
inside the oak barrel and gains all of its color and much of its flavor.

152
00:08:30,421 --> 00:08:34,920
When you char the barrel, of course it will darken, it'll turn a dark black, but it will

153
00:08:34,921 --> 00:08:38,860
open up pores in that caramelized layer where the whiskey can actually penetrate into and

154
00:08:38,861 --> 00:08:43,200
extract those wood sugars out. At least half of the flavor of Jack

155
00:08:43,201 --> 00:08:47,600
Daniels comes from the wood. Once mature, the whiskey will end

156
00:08:47,601 --> 00:08:53,340
up in one of three Jack Daniels products, Gentleman Jack, the Standard Black Label,

157
00:08:53,341 --> 00:08:58,640
or single barrel. Black Label and Gentleman Jack

158
00:08:58,641 --> 00:09:01,540
both contain a mixture of many barrels.

159
00:09:01,541 --> 00:09:06,180
But the single barrel is just that, whiskey from one barrel.

160
00:09:06,181 --> 00:09:08,580
Single barrels are selected from the upper hottest part

161
00:09:08,581 --> 00:09:11,460
of the maturation warehouse, where the whiskey ventures

162
00:09:11,461 --> 00:09:12,460
deeper into the oak.

163
00:09:17,960 --> 00:09:22,160
What we look for, we look for color, a nice caramel color.

164
00:09:22,161 --> 00:09:29,360
content of alcohol is and the overall maturity of the whiskey.

165
00:09:29,460 --> 00:09:31,700
Selected barrels are then brought to the floor of the

166
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:34,500
single-barrel bottling facility.

167
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:39,320
It's approximately 240 bottles per barrel,

168
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:41,140
depending on the barrel.

169
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:44,120
That's around 40 cases.

170
00:09:44,220 --> 00:09:46,520
Unlike wine, once whiskey is in the bottle,

171
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:48,720
it ceases to mature.

172
00:09:48,820 --> 00:09:51,420
It will not get any better than the day it was bottled.

173
00:09:51,421 --> 00:09:53,420
In fact, once the bottle has been opened,

174
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:55,020
it may get worse.

175
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:58,520
A whiskey will oxidize just like a wine or a beer does.

176
00:09:58,620 --> 00:10:00,420
It just doesn't do it as quickly.

177
00:10:00,520 --> 00:10:01,660
It gets dull.

178
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:02,860
It gets boring.

179
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:06,560
When my whiskey bottle gets down to about 1 1⁄4 left,

180
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:08,580
I invite all my friends over, and we get together,

181
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:09,680
and we drink it.

182
00:10:09,780 --> 00:10:13,280
And it guarantees a good supply of friends.

183
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:14,780
These kinds of connoisseur issues

184
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:16,920
would have been foreign to America's first whiskey

185
00:10:17,020 --> 00:10:20,680
distillers, the colonists.

186
00:10:20,681 --> 00:10:23,520
They made whiskey for the most practical of reasons,

187
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:26,120
to prevent their excess grains from spoiling.

188
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:28,620
It also concentrates the grain.

189
00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:31,920
I mean, it makes it easier to transport

190
00:10:32,020 --> 00:10:33,820
when you turn it into whiskey.

191
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:36,220
And it got to the point where whiskey was actually

192
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:38,640
like currency.

193
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:40,640
The unregulated whiskey trade flourished

194
00:10:40,740 --> 00:10:43,940
until 1791, when the new federal government

195
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:48,040
decided to tax it to pay the debt for the Revolutionary War.

196
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:50,680
Farmers revolted against the tax.

197
00:10:50,681 --> 00:10:52,680
the excisemen away by force.

198
00:10:52,780 --> 00:10:55,160
In 1794, George Washington actually

199
00:10:55,260 --> 00:10:57,420
had to send out the militia, the troops,

200
00:10:57,520 --> 00:10:59,160
to quell the uprising.

201
00:10:59,260 --> 00:11:01,420
And he actually sent out more troops

202
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:04,320
to stop the Whiskey Rebellion than he needed to actually

203
00:11:04,420 --> 00:11:05,420
defeat the British.

204
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:10,200
And ironically, just three years after the Whiskey Rebellion,

205
00:11:10,300 --> 00:11:13,440
George Washington decided to make his own whiskey

206
00:11:13,540 --> 00:11:15,040
in Mount Vernon.

207
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:19,340
And by 1799, the year that George Washington died,

208
00:11:19,341 --> 00:11:21,880
He was the largest distiller in the United States.

209
00:11:24,680 --> 00:11:27,820
At this same time in the neighboring state of Kentucky,

210
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:30,460
Jim Beam was getting its start.

211
00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:33,760
In the bourbon category, no brand sells more.

212
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:38,020
My family started making whiskey in 1795.

213
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:41,420
Jacob Beam made his first barrel of whiskey in 1795

214
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:42,560
and sold it.

215
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:45,360
He started our family in this business.

216
00:11:45,361 --> 00:11:49,860
Seven generations later, here I am.

217
00:11:49,960 --> 00:11:52,860
The company bears the name of Fred Noe's great-grandfather.

218
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:54,860
Jim Beam was the fourth generation,

219
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:57,200
and he was the one who got us back on the map

220
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:59,800
after Prohibition.

221
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:02,720
The distillery can be found in Claremont, Kentucky,

222
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:05,620
where Faith and Whiskey manage to keep company.

223
00:12:05,720 --> 00:12:09,220
And tradition means maintaining a culture, literally.

224
00:12:09,320 --> 00:12:12,420
OK, we're going into our jug yeast cooler room right now.

225
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:14,620
This is where we're keeping our strain of jug yeast.

226
00:12:14,621 --> 00:12:16,760
that's been around since Prohibition.

227
00:12:16,860 --> 00:12:20,860
How we do this is we keep our original strain

228
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:23,560
and we inoculate our new formula with it.

229
00:12:23,660 --> 00:12:25,860
We make our new jug yeast each time.

230
00:12:25,960 --> 00:12:28,760
And that keeps our strain alive.

231
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:31,380
This yeast is the engine of the distillery.

232
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:34,480
It produces every drop of alcohol that becomes bourbon.

233
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:42,380
Quality control begins as the green arrives at the distillery.

234
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:44,620
Incoming corn must pass a batch.

235
00:12:44,621 --> 00:12:45,880
of tests.

236
00:12:45,980 --> 00:12:48,720
It can't be over 14% moisture.

237
00:12:48,820 --> 00:12:52,360
Bushel weight has got to be at least 54 pounds a bushel.

238
00:12:52,460 --> 00:12:55,360
It can't have aflatoxin in it.

239
00:12:55,460 --> 00:12:58,960
Aflatoxin is a toxin produced by molds on grain that,

240
00:12:59,060 --> 00:13:02,480
if present, would glow under this ultraviolet light.

241
00:13:02,580 --> 00:13:06,880
In addition to being toxic, it affects the taste of the whiskey.

242
00:13:06,980 --> 00:13:09,980
The grains must be mostly whole and not broken into so-called

243
00:13:10,080 --> 00:13:12,120
fines.

244
00:13:12,121 --> 00:13:14,120
Once a grain has passed inspection,

245
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:16,020
it's moved into massive bins.

246
00:13:16,120 --> 00:13:18,620
These are holding bins where we run our grains

247
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:22,660
from the holding bins into the house as needed.

248
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:24,260
From above, the distillery looks

249
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:28,360
like a small city, a huge industrial undertaking.

250
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:31,480
But for Fred Ngo, bourbon is all about family.

251
00:13:31,580 --> 00:13:35,380
You know, you kind of have to be part artist, part scientist.

252
00:13:35,480 --> 00:13:37,640
And I think my dad, Booker Ngo, was probably the best

253
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:41,180
there ever was at Maconburne.

254
00:13:41,181 --> 00:13:43,320
Booker also anticipated the growth potential

255
00:13:43,420 --> 00:13:46,880
in the high-end bourbons, the so-called ultra-premiums,

256
00:13:46,980 --> 00:13:50,920
higher proof, more mature, more expensive whiskeys.

257
00:13:51,020 --> 00:13:54,720
Dad released his Booker's bourbon in 1987,

258
00:13:54,820 --> 00:13:57,800
and that's what got the whole ultra-premium line started

259
00:13:57,900 --> 00:13:59,640
for the whole bourbon industry.

260
00:13:59,740 --> 00:14:02,460
His Booker's bourbon broke the $50 barrier

261
00:14:02,560 --> 00:14:03,840
on a bottle of bourbon.

262
00:14:03,940 --> 00:14:07,900
It's bottled straight from the barrel, uncut, unfiltered,

263
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:09,740
the way bourbon was 100 years ago.

264
00:14:09,741 --> 00:14:11,400
for people to sip and savor.

265
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:13,280
I mean, that's the thing about these bourbons.

266
00:14:13,380 --> 00:14:16,580
It takes us seven, eight, nine, ten years to make it.

267
00:14:16,680 --> 00:14:18,740
Beam makes four ultra-premiums,

268
00:14:18,840 --> 00:14:23,420
Basil Hayden's, Baker's, Booker's,

269
00:14:23,520 --> 00:14:26,460
and the best-selling ultra-premium bourbon in the world,

270
00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:28,160
Knob Creek.

271
00:14:28,260 --> 00:14:30,220
This one's named for the area down the road

272
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:32,160
where Abraham Lincoln was born.

273
00:14:32,260 --> 00:14:36,200
Now, this bourbon stays in these barrels for nine years.

274
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:38,740
So storage is an issue.

275
00:14:38,741 --> 00:14:42,980
Beam matures 1,800,000 barrels in 55 rack houses.

276
00:14:45,980 --> 00:14:47,980
The process of removing whiskey from the barrels,

277
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:50,280
also known as dumping, takes place,

278
00:14:50,380 --> 00:14:54,180
not surprisingly, in the dump room.

279
00:14:54,280 --> 00:14:56,000
He's pulling the bung out of the barrel.

280
00:14:56,100 --> 00:14:58,500
What that does, that enables to put the vent tube

281
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:01,100
in the barrel so we can get the whiskey out.

282
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:02,840
That whiskey's removed from the barrel

283
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:05,540
and goes straight into the trough, through the piping,

284
00:15:05,640 --> 00:15:06,640
into a tank.

285
00:15:08,740 --> 00:15:11,080
Once empty, the barrels are sold.

286
00:15:11,180 --> 00:15:13,680
They can only be used once for bourbon.

287
00:15:13,780 --> 00:15:14,940
We get done with these barrels.

288
00:15:15,040 --> 00:15:17,580
We sell a lot of them to our friends over in Scotland

289
00:15:17,680 --> 00:15:18,820
to make the scotch.

290
00:15:18,920 --> 00:15:21,980
Scotch is OK because it's got some of my bourbon in the barrels

291
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:23,980
we sell them.

292
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:26,120
For Fred Ngo, sampling bourbon straight out

293
00:15:26,220 --> 00:15:29,160
of the barrel is his favorite part of the job.

294
00:15:29,260 --> 00:15:31,160
This is what we call a thief.

295
00:15:31,260 --> 00:15:33,160
This is how we steal a little drink of liquor

296
00:15:33,260 --> 00:15:34,440
out of these barrels.

297
00:15:34,540 --> 00:15:35,460
Just drop it in here.

298
00:15:35,560 --> 00:15:36,760
There's a hole on top.

299
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:38,740
Put my thumb on it good and tight.

300
00:15:38,741 --> 00:15:40,740
And then I'll bring it out of here,

301
00:15:40,741 --> 00:15:43,740
and then I'll get a little shot of liquor.

302
00:15:49,740 --> 00:15:51,740
Mm-mm.

303
00:15:51,741 --> 00:15:53,740
What I'm doing there is the Kentucky Chew.

304
00:15:53,741 --> 00:15:56,740
Put the bourbon in your mouth and chew on it a little bit.

305
00:15:56,741 --> 00:15:59,740
And what it does, it involves your entire mouth

306
00:15:59,741 --> 00:16:01,740
and the tasting.

307
00:16:01,741 --> 00:16:03,740
For those who take their bourbon seriously,

308
00:16:03,741 --> 00:16:05,740
these are good times.

309
00:16:05,741 --> 00:16:07,740
People are drinking less,

310
00:16:07,741 --> 00:16:09,540
and they want to drink better.

311
00:16:09,640 --> 00:16:14,180
It's got the bourbon business rocking and rolling again.

312
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:15,980
While business is booming for distillers

313
00:16:16,080 --> 00:16:18,480
in the American South, an ocean away

314
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:21,380
in the birthplace of whiskey, a liquid gold rush

315
00:16:21,480 --> 00:16:22,480
is going on as well.

316
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:28,300
Dublin, one of the fastest growing and most youthful

317
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:31,400
capitals in Europe, is experiencing a renaissance.

318
00:16:31,500 --> 00:16:32,800
We were done.

319
00:16:32,900 --> 00:16:33,700
Sorry.

320
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:36,700
Jameson, founded in 1780, like Dublin,

321
00:16:36,701 --> 00:16:40,980
Dublin is in the midst of its own resurgence.

322
00:16:40,981 --> 00:16:44,780
10 years ago, we would have distilled somewhere in the order

323
00:16:44,781 --> 00:16:49,280
of maybe 12 million liters of pure alcohol per annum.

324
00:16:49,281 --> 00:16:51,840
Currently, we're distilling 25 million liters.

325
00:16:51,841 --> 00:16:54,060
It's a very exciting time to be involved

326
00:16:54,061 --> 00:16:56,520
on the Jemson story in Ireland.

327
00:16:56,521 --> 00:16:57,960
Part of this excitement is coming

328
00:16:57,961 --> 00:17:00,320
from reaching a new, younger customer.

329
00:17:00,321 --> 00:17:02,760
It would have been the case not too many years ago

330
00:17:02,761 --> 00:17:04,600
that they would have regarded whiskey as maybe

331
00:17:04,601 --> 00:17:08,640
an older person's beverage, and that is not the case with Jamison.

332
00:17:08,641 --> 00:17:12,700
Jamison's smooth taste profile is key to its growing appeal.

333
00:17:12,701 --> 00:17:16,740
The best whiskeys evolve on the palate as you're tasting it.

334
00:17:16,741 --> 00:17:18,780
And this is the Jamison 18-year-old,

335
00:17:18,781 --> 00:17:24,120
and this is an outstanding blended Irish whiskey.

336
00:17:24,121 --> 00:17:28,860
It's a nice silky texture to it, lots of toffee and fruit,

337
00:17:28,861 --> 00:17:32,960
a little bit of nuttiness, some of that cereal grain.

338
00:17:32,961 --> 00:17:38,860
It's continuing to evolve, fading on to a nice, soft, malty finish.

339
00:17:38,861 --> 00:17:41,160
This accessible yet complex blended whiskey

340
00:17:41,161 --> 00:17:43,260
is produced by an Irish whiskey industry

341
00:17:43,261 --> 00:17:46,700
that is highly consolidated.

342
00:17:46,701 --> 00:17:49,280
The island has only three major distilleries,

343
00:17:49,281 --> 00:17:52,040
Bushmills, Cooley, and Middleton,

344
00:17:52,041 --> 00:17:55,620
but they produce roughly 30 different whiskeys.

345
00:17:55,621 --> 00:17:57,620
Jamison's old distillery in Middleton,

346
00:17:57,621 --> 00:18:01,480
dating back to the 1820s, is now a visitor center

347
00:18:01,481 --> 00:18:04,680
and serves as a graceful facade for the very industrial guts

348
00:18:04,681 --> 00:18:09,660
of the operation, the working distillery built in the 1970s.

349
00:18:09,661 --> 00:18:13,640
Two grains fuel this concrete and steel behemoth, corn distilled

350
00:18:13,641 --> 00:18:17,060
into a pure grain alcohol almost like a vodka,

351
00:18:17,061 --> 00:18:19,360
and for flavor, barley.

352
00:18:19,361 --> 00:18:22,400
It gives it a very smooth flavor on the palate,

353
00:18:22,401 --> 00:18:26,760
and it also gives it like a silky and serum-y mouthfeel.

354
00:18:26,761 --> 00:18:30,580
And that's what the barley does in the mash.

355
00:18:30,581 --> 00:18:33,520
To provide the sugars necessary for fermentation,

356
00:18:33,521 --> 00:18:35,160
the barley has been soaked in water

357
00:18:35,161 --> 00:18:37,880
in order to induce it to malt or germinate.

358
00:18:37,881 --> 00:18:41,160
What you're doing is really fooling the barley grain

359
00:18:41,161 --> 00:18:44,120
into thinking it's going to grow up into a barley plant.

360
00:18:44,121 --> 00:18:48,200
So you're allowing it to access the starchy reservoir

361
00:18:48,201 --> 00:18:50,340
within the grain.

362
00:18:50,341 --> 00:18:51,860
Once these sugars have been extracted

363
00:18:51,861 --> 00:18:54,000
from the malted barley in the brew house,

364
00:18:54,001 --> 00:18:57,680
they are pumped into gigantic fermentation vats.

365
00:18:57,681 --> 00:19:03,020
Up here, we have our fermentation vats, 32 in total,

366
00:19:03,021 --> 00:19:07,820
each containing 200,000 liters of fermenting beer

367
00:19:07,821 --> 00:19:09,500
or fermenting wash.

368
00:19:09,501 --> 00:19:12,120
Yeast is added, and it is here that the yeast

369
00:19:12,121 --> 00:19:16,060
will convert with that rich sugary liquid into alcohol

370
00:19:16,061 --> 00:19:21,240
and also into a whole range of fantastic flavors.

371
00:19:21,241 --> 00:19:23,740
This is the still house of the Jamison Distillery.

372
00:19:23,741 --> 00:19:26,340
Behind me here, we have the first pot still,

373
00:19:26,341 --> 00:19:30,740
The alcoholic vapors, along with all of the volatile flavor compounds,

374
00:19:31,080 --> 00:19:34,060
rise up through the neck of the still, across the linearm,

375
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:36,580
and gets condensed back into a liquid again.

376
00:19:36,940 --> 00:19:39,040
This liquid is known as low wines,

377
00:19:39,041 --> 00:19:42,640
and it contains about 20 to 40% alcohol by volume.

378
00:19:43,100 --> 00:19:45,840
This low wines is then pumped into the second pot still here.

379
00:19:46,460 --> 00:19:47,660
Again, heat is applied.

380
00:19:48,040 --> 00:19:50,640
The alcoholic vapors rise up through the neck of the still.

381
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:55,220
The strength at this stage has been increased to about 70% alcohol by volume

382
00:19:55,221 --> 00:19:59,420
is then taken and put into the third still.

383
00:19:59,520 --> 00:20:01,260
Triple distillation has always been

384
00:20:01,360 --> 00:20:03,560
type of distillation used in Ireland.

385
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:06,360
And that's because it produces a particular character

386
00:20:06,460 --> 00:20:08,660
and a particular style of distillate.

387
00:20:08,760 --> 00:20:12,780
What it really does is that it removes some of the heaviness

388
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:15,980
to allow you to push the flavor spectrum more

389
00:20:16,080 --> 00:20:19,520
into the floral and fruity, spicy, and fragrant

390
00:20:19,620 --> 00:20:20,820
characteristics.

391
00:20:20,920 --> 00:20:22,620
The Jameson blend is a combination

392
00:20:22,621 --> 00:20:26,860
of these pot still whiskeys and lighter grain whiskey.

393
00:20:26,960 --> 00:20:30,120
Grain whiskey, it's essentially from corn,

394
00:20:30,220 --> 00:20:33,860
what we call maize, distilled in column stilts.

395
00:20:33,960 --> 00:20:37,260
So it comes off the still at quite a high alcoholic strength,

396
00:20:37,360 --> 00:20:40,880
and it's got a lighter style.

397
00:20:40,980 --> 00:20:43,280
This lighter whiskey is aged in bourbon barrels

398
00:20:43,380 --> 00:20:45,280
that have already been used several times.

399
00:20:45,281 --> 00:20:56,620
Now here we see inside of what a bourbon bar looks like.

400
00:20:56,720 --> 00:20:58,620
Whiskey barrels are porous.

401
00:20:58,720 --> 00:21:01,320
In the summer months, as the whiskey warms up,

402
00:21:01,420 --> 00:21:04,000
pressure builds inside the barrel.

403
00:21:04,100 --> 00:21:07,360
The whiskey penetrates the oak, absorbing both the wood's flavors

404
00:21:07,460 --> 00:21:09,980
and its amber color.

405
00:21:10,080 --> 00:21:12,640
It's more than just a maturation,

406
00:21:12,641 --> 00:21:15,780
but there's almost like a melding of flavors.

407
00:21:15,781 --> 00:21:19,120
The charred oak is saturated with 20 pounds of bourbon.

408
00:21:19,121 --> 00:21:22,780
Both oak and bourbon contribute to Jameson's flavor.

409
00:21:22,781 --> 00:21:27,920
We don't use any virgin oak in our whiskey production,

410
00:21:27,921 --> 00:21:31,960
because the heavy extracts from the virgin oak

411
00:21:31,961 --> 00:21:34,560
will be too heavy for the light distillate

412
00:21:34,561 --> 00:21:37,440
that we produce here in Middleton.

413
00:21:37,441 --> 00:21:40,700
Pot still whiskey is also aged in a refill oak barrel.

414
00:21:40,701 --> 00:21:42,960
It's one that's been used two or three times.

415
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:45,880
And the whiskey that comes out of such a barrel

416
00:21:45,881 --> 00:21:49,860
allows more of the distillate character to be retained.

417
00:21:51,620 --> 00:21:55,340
But Jameson ages some of its whiskey in sherry casks,

418
00:21:55,760 --> 00:21:58,420
which are two and a half times bigger than standard oak barrels.

419
00:21:58,960 --> 00:22:04,460
You can take that same whiskey and mature it in a sherry cask.

420
00:22:04,700 --> 00:22:06,780
And you can see here that the color is quite different.

421
00:22:06,781 --> 00:22:12,860
And that's because Spanish oak has a higher level of oak extract and tannin.

422
00:22:12,861 --> 00:22:17,760
The sherry butts are very much different from our bourbon barrel.

423
00:22:18,160 --> 00:22:26,280
They're seasoned with sherry wine for two and a half years before they come home to be used here in the distillery.

424
00:22:26,540 --> 00:22:35,140
The sherry gives you a nutty taste, honey notes, gives you a nice, deep, chestnutty colour to the spirit.

425
00:22:35,141 --> 00:22:37,180
in our warehouses here in Middleton,

426
00:22:37,280 --> 00:22:43,140
we would have circa 650,000 casks.

427
00:22:43,240 --> 00:22:48,380
We are filling approximately 175,000 casks a year,

428
00:22:48,480 --> 00:22:54,400
and we're dumping about 130,000 casks a year.

429
00:22:54,500 --> 00:22:56,700
But not all of the whiskey that goes into the barrels

430
00:22:56,800 --> 00:22:59,000
will make it into a bottle because of what's

431
00:22:59,100 --> 00:23:00,840
known as the angel's share.

432
00:23:00,940 --> 00:23:03,840
When the alcohol is expanding, it's pushing out

433
00:23:03,841 --> 00:23:06,600
out into the yolk and evaporating off.

434
00:23:06,700 --> 00:23:15,180
So we lose about 2.2%, 2.3% per annum to the angel's share.

435
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:16,980
This most modern and sophisticated

436
00:23:17,080 --> 00:23:19,980
of products has roots in Irish monasteries,

437
00:23:20,080 --> 00:23:24,700
like this one at Glindalough in County Wicklow.

438
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:26,800
Irish monks made whiskey at sites like these

439
00:23:26,900 --> 00:23:29,840
perhaps as early as 1,000 years ago.

440
00:23:29,940 --> 00:23:32,040
Whiskey was invented here in Ireland.

441
00:23:32,041 --> 00:23:36,080
Missionary monks from Ireland went out to preach Christianity in Europe.

442
00:23:36,820 --> 00:23:42,600
They discovered in the east an alembic, a pot still, a distillation vessel.

443
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:45,580
And they brought this back to Ireland.

444
00:23:46,040 --> 00:23:47,460
It had been used for perfume.

445
00:23:47,920 --> 00:23:49,820
They found a much better use for it.

446
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:52,540
And they began to distill whiskey.

447
00:23:53,140 --> 00:23:56,320
Whiskey, they called it with the Gaelic name ishgabaha.

448
00:23:56,980 --> 00:23:58,880
Ishgabaha, the water of life.

449
00:23:58,881 --> 00:24:03,080
Today, Yshkabaha, or whiskey, is being produced in quantities

450
00:24:03,180 --> 00:24:06,680
the Irish monks could never have imagined.

451
00:24:06,780 --> 00:24:08,920
At Jamieson, once the whiskey has matured,

452
00:24:09,020 --> 00:24:11,120
the finished product is shipped from the distillery

453
00:24:11,220 --> 00:24:13,620
in Middleton to Dublin for bottling.

454
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:15,120
Here at the Jamieson bottling plant,

455
00:24:15,220 --> 00:24:18,140
we're running all week, every week of the year.

456
00:24:18,240 --> 00:24:21,040
We're producing about a million bottles a week,

457
00:24:21,140 --> 00:24:24,140
in total between 40 and 50 million bottles every year.

458
00:24:24,141 --> 00:24:30,380
every year. In Ireland, a single distillery

459
00:24:30,381 --> 00:24:33,980
produces a variety of whiskeys for its own blends.

460
00:24:33,981 --> 00:24:37,020
But in Scotland, competing distilleries have developed a

461
00:24:37,021 --> 00:24:42,060
surprising system to achieve their complex blends.

462
00:24:45,660 --> 00:24:49,180
Scotland is a small country with a giant presence in the global

463
00:24:49,181 --> 00:24:52,700
whiskey industry. It has roughly 90 distilleries,

464
00:24:52,701 --> 00:24:56,100
than any other whiskey-producing country on Earth.

465
00:24:56,200 --> 00:24:58,600
And whiskey is Scotland's largest export,

466
00:24:58,700 --> 00:25:02,440
grossing almost $6 billion per year.

467
00:25:02,540 --> 00:25:05,340
It was global interest in Scotland's single malt scotches

468
00:25:05,440 --> 00:25:09,180
in the 1980s and 1990s that revived a whiskey category

469
00:25:09,280 --> 00:25:11,760
that had been in decline for decades.

470
00:25:11,860 --> 00:25:14,720
Scotland's single malt scotches are some of the most expensive,

471
00:25:14,820 --> 00:25:16,560
sought-after whiskeys in the world.

472
00:25:22,700 --> 00:25:26,300
Single means it's the product of just one distillery.

473
00:25:26,400 --> 00:25:30,300
Malt means that it's made exclusively for malted barley.

474
00:25:30,400 --> 00:25:34,740
And scotch means it has to be distilled and matured in scull

475
00:25:34,840 --> 00:25:37,340
and in oak barrels for a minimum of three years.

476
00:25:37,440 --> 00:25:38,940
Here at the Glenlivet Distillery,

477
00:25:39,040 --> 00:25:40,760
we only produce one whiskey, and that

478
00:25:40,860 --> 00:25:42,660
is the Glenlivet single malt.

479
00:25:42,760 --> 00:25:47,760
The Glenlivet is the world's number two single malt whiskey.

480
00:25:47,860 --> 00:25:49,600
A single malt like the Glenlivet

481
00:25:49,700 --> 00:25:52,700
may be sold on its own or as part of a company

482
00:25:52,701 --> 00:25:56,160
of blended whisky, like Chivas Regal.

483
00:25:56,161 --> 00:25:59,000
Despite Scotland's reputation for single malts,

484
00:25:59,001 --> 00:26:02,160
90% of its whiskies are sold as blends, which are more

485
00:26:02,161 --> 00:26:05,260
accessible to the tastes of the average consumer.

486
00:26:05,261 --> 00:26:07,260
When we create a blended whisky,

487
00:26:07,261 --> 00:26:10,340
you have to use about 30 single malts.

488
00:26:10,341 --> 00:26:14,340
So we're constantly exchanging whisky with some of our

489
00:26:14,341 --> 00:26:16,940
competitor companies.

490
00:26:16,941 --> 00:26:19,860
Most Scottish distilleries only distill one scotch,

491
00:26:19,861 --> 00:26:21,860
their own single malt.

492
00:26:21,861 --> 00:26:23,920
they must rely on other distilleries to contribute

493
00:26:24,020 --> 00:26:26,500
Scotches to their blend.

494
00:26:26,600 --> 00:26:29,500
There's a great deal of diversity to Scotch whiskeys.

495
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:33,200
There are four recognized whisky making regions.

496
00:26:33,300 --> 00:26:34,900
Firstly, in the south of Scotland,

497
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:36,700
an area known as the Lowlands.

498
00:26:36,800 --> 00:26:38,320
And the whiskies from the Lowlands

499
00:26:38,420 --> 00:26:42,920
tend to be very soft and gentle in their characteristic.

500
00:26:43,020 --> 00:26:45,820
Then we have a little island on the west coast of Scotland

501
00:26:45,920 --> 00:26:47,520
known as Islay.

502
00:26:47,620 --> 00:26:51,620
And the whiskies from Islay are very smoky, very

503
00:26:51,621 --> 00:26:55,620
very pungent, very distinctive.

504
00:26:55,720 --> 00:26:58,660
The West Coast whiskeys get their smoke from peat.

505
00:26:58,760 --> 00:27:01,560
The distilleries on the West Coast traditionally

506
00:27:01,660 --> 00:27:04,560
have always used peat to dry the malted barley.

507
00:27:04,660 --> 00:27:06,560
And when you burn the peat, it gives off

508
00:27:06,660 --> 00:27:08,380
a very distinctive smoke.

509
00:27:08,480 --> 00:27:11,680
This clings to the malted barley, and in turn,

510
00:27:11,780 --> 00:27:13,580
it flavors the whiskey.

511
00:27:13,680 --> 00:27:17,480
Then we have a huge geographic region known as the Highlands.

512
00:27:17,580 --> 00:27:19,280
And the malt whiskeys from the Highlands

513
00:27:19,281 --> 00:27:24,020
tend to be fruity and spicy in their characteristic.

514
00:27:24,120 --> 00:27:27,380
Here in Speyside, the greatest of all of the four whiskey

515
00:27:27,480 --> 00:27:29,920
making regions, the whiskies from Speyside

516
00:27:30,020 --> 00:27:32,920
tend to be sweet and floral and fruity

517
00:27:33,020 --> 00:27:35,520
in their characteristics.

518
00:27:35,620 --> 00:27:37,660
Hundreds of years ago, the Speyside region

519
00:27:37,760 --> 00:27:40,740
was known as a haven for illegal distillers.

520
00:27:40,840 --> 00:27:46,300
After the Battle of Culloden in 1746, where the English heavily

521
00:27:46,400 --> 00:27:49,280
defeated the Scots, the English taxed

522
00:27:49,281 --> 00:27:53,320
the making of Scotch whiskey to such an extent that the Scots

523
00:27:53,420 --> 00:27:55,220
couldn't make it legally.

524
00:27:55,320 --> 00:27:58,520
So they all began making it illegally.

525
00:27:58,620 --> 00:28:00,820
They used portable stills like this one that

526
00:28:00,920 --> 00:28:02,720
could be moved if soldiers showed up.

527
00:28:02,820 --> 00:28:04,820
This is the size of still that you would have saw the illegal

528
00:28:04,920 --> 00:28:05,940
distillers using.

529
00:28:06,040 --> 00:28:08,940
It would have been filled with that mixture of sweet liquids,

530
00:28:09,040 --> 00:28:12,340
the wort that they used as the first stage of the process.

531
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:14,580
It would have been boiled up inside here.

532
00:28:14,680 --> 00:28:17,080
And then you'd have watched the vapors coming over here

533
00:28:17,081 --> 00:28:19,080
As it goes down through the coil in the cold water,

534
00:28:19,180 --> 00:28:21,580
it's going to condense back into a liquid again

535
00:28:21,680 --> 00:28:25,820
before appearing out the funnel at the end.

536
00:28:25,920 --> 00:28:29,820
Today, water is still key to the operation of the distillery.

537
00:28:29,920 --> 00:28:32,620
We'll use probably in the region of 3 million liters

538
00:28:32,720 --> 00:28:37,040
of water a day in between mashing, cooling, condensing.

539
00:28:37,140 --> 00:28:39,900
The distillery also consumes 40 tons of malted

540
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:43,080
or germinated barley a day.

541
00:28:43,180 --> 00:28:46,080
Glenlivet is made from 100% barley.

542
00:28:46,081 --> 00:28:47,620
And this is the mash tun.

543
00:28:47,720 --> 00:28:49,720
And this is where we start to extract the sugars

544
00:28:49,820 --> 00:28:52,720
and the starches that we're going to make into alcohol.

545
00:28:52,820 --> 00:28:56,420
And the way we do that is we fill 12 tons of grist

546
00:28:56,520 --> 00:28:59,120
into this vessel.

547
00:28:59,220 --> 00:29:01,420
Hot water extracts the sugars.

548
00:29:01,520 --> 00:29:03,640
And then this liquid, the wort,

549
00:29:03,740 --> 00:29:06,540
is pumped into the fermenters.

550
00:29:06,640 --> 00:29:09,100
Into each of these, 60,000 liters of liquid

551
00:29:09,200 --> 00:29:11,480
will come through from the mash tun.

552
00:29:11,580 --> 00:29:14,580
And then we will add 215 kilos of yeast.

553
00:29:14,581 --> 00:29:18,380
And that creates a 100% natural process.

554
00:29:18,480 --> 00:29:21,220
So this is where we create the alcohol.

555
00:29:21,320 --> 00:29:23,960
After two days, the 8.5% beer is

556
00:29:24,060 --> 00:29:25,960
ready for double distillation.

557
00:29:26,060 --> 00:29:28,460
That's very similar to Jameson's triple distillation

558
00:29:28,560 --> 00:29:30,860
process.

559
00:29:30,960 --> 00:29:32,560
Surprisingly, the entire distillery

560
00:29:32,660 --> 00:29:38,600
is monitored by only two people and banks of computers.

561
00:29:38,700 --> 00:29:41,100
Though computers have a large hand in making it,

562
00:29:41,200 --> 00:29:44,580
the Glenlivet has a sensual appeal.

563
00:29:44,581 --> 00:29:52,340
Wonderfully soft, wonderfully rounded and rich on the nose.

564
00:29:56,420 --> 00:29:58,480
And it just is beautiful.

565
00:29:58,680 --> 00:30:02,600
It just goes down so gently, warming all the way.

566
00:30:03,640 --> 00:30:06,340
On a cold morning here at Glenlivet Distillery,

567
00:30:06,920 --> 00:30:09,180
this is central heating as it should be.

568
00:30:11,160 --> 00:30:14,320
Single malt scotches are the world's most expensive whiskeys.

569
00:30:14,580 --> 00:30:18,820
Glenlivet's range from their 12-year-old, which costs $35,

570
00:30:18,920 --> 00:30:23,580
to the Glenlivet 25-year-old, which will set you back $350,

571
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:27,460
to the Glenlivet Cellar Collection 1964 limited edition,

572
00:30:27,560 --> 00:30:33,960
with only 1,824 bottles produced, which goes for a tidy $2,000

573
00:30:34,060 --> 00:30:36,260
a bottle.

574
00:30:36,360 --> 00:30:38,040
And scotch prices for rare bottles

575
00:30:38,140 --> 00:30:40,180
just go up from there.

576
00:30:40,280 --> 00:30:42,700
One of the world's most expensive bottles of whiskey,

577
00:30:42,701 --> 00:30:47,700
a 1926 Macallan Scotch was purchased for $75,000

578
00:30:47,800 --> 00:30:50,200
in Seoul, South Korea.

579
00:30:50,300 --> 00:30:53,540
How does a bottle of Scotch command this kind of price tag?

580
00:30:53,640 --> 00:30:56,940
Rarity, number one, is remarkably important.

581
00:30:57,040 --> 00:31:00,160
Are you drinking, in fact, a piece of history?

582
00:31:00,260 --> 00:31:03,760
Two is the historical significance.

583
00:31:03,860 --> 00:31:07,860
If we're going to drink that old, did this define a category

584
00:31:07,960 --> 00:31:09,260
or did it not?

585
00:31:09,261 --> 00:31:13,960
Why are people buying these $300, $400, $500 and up whiskeys?

586
00:31:14,060 --> 00:31:16,760
It's a lot easier to go out and buy a $300 bottle of whiskey

587
00:31:16,860 --> 00:31:21,700
than it is a $40,000 sports car or a $600,000 vacation home.

588
00:31:21,800 --> 00:31:24,300
You know, it's an affordable luxury.

589
00:31:24,400 --> 00:31:27,220
It's a beautiful thing.

590
00:31:27,320 --> 00:31:29,220
The Scotch whiskey distillers historically

591
00:31:29,320 --> 00:31:30,920
had their struggles with the law.

592
00:31:31,020 --> 00:31:33,620
In this country just north of the United States,

593
00:31:33,720 --> 00:31:37,520
distillers flourished because of the law, prohibition.

594
00:31:40,260 --> 00:31:43,260
A country's most prized products often reflect the personality

595
00:31:43,360 --> 00:31:45,260
of its people.

596
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:48,260
Canadian whiskeys, mostly blends of rye and neutral grain

597
00:31:48,360 --> 00:31:52,100
spirits, underscore the easy nature of both Canada and its

598
00:31:52,200 --> 00:31:53,700
whiskey.

599
00:31:53,800 --> 00:31:58,220
Canadian whiskey is probably the lightest and most delicate and

600
00:31:58,320 --> 00:32:01,620
easiest to embrace whiskey of all the major whiskey-producing

601
00:32:01,720 --> 00:32:04,260
countries.

602
00:32:04,360 --> 00:32:07,260
Canada's distillers, the world's number three producers

603
00:32:07,261 --> 00:32:10,660
of whiskey, make almost 48 million gallons of whiskey

604
00:32:10,760 --> 00:32:11,560
per year.

605
00:32:14,860 --> 00:32:18,800
The Canadian national style originated in 1884 with the

606
00:32:18,900 --> 00:32:21,800
introduction of one of the country's best known whiskeys,

607
00:32:21,900 --> 00:32:26,220
Canadian Club. The Canadian Club classic 12-year-old is

608
00:32:26,320 --> 00:32:28,620
the company's most mature expression of its flavor

609
00:32:28,720 --> 00:32:32,560
profile. What we do get is vanilla,

610
00:32:32,660 --> 00:32:35,720
and lots of it. We do get some of that grainy

611
00:32:35,721 --> 00:32:37,720
that grainy character from the rye.

612
00:32:41,220 --> 00:32:45,720
That is all the way through an enjoyable, pleasant drink.

613
00:32:48,560 --> 00:32:51,760
The lightness of Canadian Club comes from its base whiskey,

614
00:32:51,860 --> 00:32:54,180
a corn distillate with no color.

615
00:32:54,280 --> 00:32:57,480
It may comprise as much as 80% to 90% of the volume.

616
00:32:57,580 --> 00:33:00,620
And unlike US bourbons, has almost all of the corn's

617
00:33:00,720 --> 00:33:03,820
grainy flavor distilled out of it.

618
00:33:03,821 --> 00:33:06,220
The whiskey blends of other countries, like Scotland and

619
00:33:06,221 --> 00:33:09,320
Ireland, contain less of this neutral-based whiskey and tend

620
00:33:09,321 --> 00:33:12,220
to have a more aggressive taste.

621
00:33:12,221 --> 00:33:14,860
Canadian Club's flavoring component is distilled from

622
00:33:14,861 --> 00:33:18,760
fermented rye, rye malt, and barley malt.

623
00:33:18,761 --> 00:33:21,880
To be called a Canadian whiskey, the government has set forth

624
00:33:21,881 --> 00:33:24,880
rules and regulations. For example, we have to use a

625
00:33:24,881 --> 00:33:28,980
variety of different grains. It has to be manufactured and

626
00:33:28,981 --> 00:33:32,080
aged here in Canada for a minimum of three years.

627
00:33:32,081 --> 00:33:34,740
And similar to the Scottish and Irish distillers,

628
00:33:34,741 --> 00:33:37,220
we're able to use our barrels several times.

629
00:33:40,380 --> 00:33:42,020
At Canadian Club, those barrels

630
00:33:42,021 --> 00:33:46,820
are once-used bourbon barrels purchased from the US.

631
00:33:46,821 --> 00:33:50,140
We Canadians get to use our barrels several times,

632
00:33:50,141 --> 00:33:54,540
therefore attributing to a lighter oak flavor profile

633
00:33:54,541 --> 00:33:56,840
product.

634
00:33:56,841 --> 00:33:59,980
The Canadian Club line features four different whiskeys.

635
00:33:59,981 --> 00:34:02,480
There's our standard six-year-old Canadian Club,

636
00:34:02,580 --> 00:34:05,680
which is available in 151 countries around the world.

637
00:34:06,240 --> 00:34:10,020
CC Reserve, our 10-year-old format, is our premium version.

638
00:34:10,760 --> 00:34:13,720
The super premium, Classic 12 by Canadian Club,

639
00:34:13,880 --> 00:34:16,480
and, of course, CC Sherry Cask.

640
00:34:19,780 --> 00:34:22,780
Canadian Club owes its tradition of elegant whiskeys

641
00:34:22,781 --> 00:34:24,600
to its founder, Hiram Walker.

642
00:34:25,480 --> 00:34:28,320
He worked in what is now the Canadian Club Heritage Centre.

643
00:34:28,321 --> 00:34:31,320
So we're now in the Canadian Club's founding father's

644
00:34:31,420 --> 00:34:32,960
office, Hiram Walker.

645
00:34:33,060 --> 00:34:37,360
Mr. Hiram Walker came to Canada in 1856.

646
00:34:37,460 --> 00:34:41,260
And in 1858, he opened the distillery.

647
00:34:41,360 --> 00:34:43,060
This is actually Hiram Walker's desk,

648
00:34:43,160 --> 00:34:46,180
where he used to conduct business on a daily basis.

649
00:34:46,280 --> 00:34:50,080
A US citizen, Walker opened his distillery in Windsor,

650
00:34:50,180 --> 00:34:53,920
Ontario, across the river from his home in Detroit, because he

651
00:34:54,020 --> 00:34:57,580
feared the growing power of the temperance movement in the US.

652
00:34:57,581 --> 00:35:01,420
He may have been off by 60 years, but in 1920,

653
00:35:01,480 --> 00:35:04,380
when Prohibition hit the US, Walker's sons

654
00:35:04,460 --> 00:35:06,120
had inherited the company.

655
00:35:06,180 --> 00:35:08,700
They found eager distributors of their Canadian whiskey,

656
00:35:08,760 --> 00:35:11,160
the Purple Gang, who were mostly Jewish mobsters

657
00:35:11,220 --> 00:35:15,100
out of Detroit, as well as the Chicago Mob.

658
00:35:15,160 --> 00:35:19,000
When Prohibition entered into the US in 1920,

659
00:35:19,060 --> 00:35:22,000
the boys from the Purple Gang out of downtown Detroit

660
00:35:22,080 --> 00:35:23,900
would come over here to conduct business.

661
00:35:23,980 --> 00:35:26,540
Mr. Capone himself would conduct business at this table

662
00:35:26,541 --> 00:35:28,540
table with the Hiram Walker boys.

663
00:35:28,640 --> 00:35:31,120
And this became known as the speakeasy, which is still

664
00:35:31,220 --> 00:35:33,820
what we're using it for today.

665
00:35:33,920 --> 00:35:36,380
The mile-wide Detroit River separates Detroit

666
00:35:36,480 --> 00:35:37,980
from Windsor, Ontario.

667
00:35:38,080 --> 00:35:40,480
During prohibition, smugglers made the short trip across

668
00:35:40,580 --> 00:35:43,300
to Canada, loaded up with Canadian Club,

669
00:35:43,400 --> 00:35:46,860
and then distributed the product to a thirsty US populace.

670
00:35:46,960 --> 00:35:50,400
People came across the Detroit River by boat.

671
00:35:50,500 --> 00:35:55,360
Or during winter freezes, it became an ice road for smugglers.

672
00:35:55,361 --> 00:35:58,000
The sales of Canadian Club rose drastically

673
00:35:58,100 --> 00:36:00,600
during prohibition.

674
00:36:00,700 --> 00:36:03,160
But since mobsters had a hand in distribution,

675
00:36:03,260 --> 00:36:05,100
shipments were often hijacked.

676
00:36:05,200 --> 00:36:07,240
A system of coded telegrams was developed

677
00:36:07,340 --> 00:36:09,000
to throw thieves off the trail.

678
00:36:09,100 --> 00:36:11,880
This one's dated July 31, 1931.

679
00:36:11,980 --> 00:36:13,780
Just above it, they un-coded it.

680
00:36:13,880 --> 00:36:18,020
We have 300 cases of club have left on its way to London.

681
00:36:18,120 --> 00:36:20,160
So this became a very, very popular way

682
00:36:20,260 --> 00:36:23,120
of transporting product.

683
00:36:23,121 --> 00:36:26,360
One Canadian club bottle known as the Prohibition bottle

684
00:36:26,460 --> 00:36:28,360
was a favorite with drinkers.

685
00:36:28,460 --> 00:36:29,760
It's concave in the back.

686
00:36:29,860 --> 00:36:31,560
The guys would slip it into their pockets,

687
00:36:31,660 --> 00:36:33,260
and the ladies were strapping it on their leg,

688
00:36:33,360 --> 00:36:35,060
and this bottle kind of hugs your body.

689
00:36:35,160 --> 00:36:37,760
Another name for this bottle was known as the bootlegger

690
00:36:37,860 --> 00:36:38,960
bottle.

691
00:36:39,060 --> 00:36:42,180
The reason for that is ladies back then used to wear long gowns,

692
00:36:42,280 --> 00:36:43,980
and the boots had straps in them.

693
00:36:44,080 --> 00:36:46,480
They would strap these bottles to their leg.

694
00:36:46,580 --> 00:36:50,380
Thus came the term bootlegger.

695
00:36:50,480 --> 00:36:53,100
Canadian clubs' whiskeys were known as a

696
00:36:53,120 --> 00:36:56,320
as they are now for being cocktail friendly.

697
00:36:56,420 --> 00:36:58,120
My favorite cocktail is what I've called

698
00:36:58,220 --> 00:37:00,520
the Millionaire's Manhattan, a measure and a half

699
00:37:00,620 --> 00:37:02,760
of Canadian Club classic 12-year-old.

700
00:37:02,860 --> 00:37:08,060
Next, add a half a measure of Harvey's Bristol cream.

701
00:37:08,160 --> 00:37:10,300
And last but not least, garnish it with a couple

702
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:11,500
of maraschino cherries.

703
00:37:14,140 --> 00:37:16,640
The Millionaire's Manhattan.

704
00:37:16,740 --> 00:37:19,440
Canadian whiskeys express a distinct national character

705
00:37:19,540 --> 00:37:23,120
that has little in common with US whiskeys.

706
00:37:23,121 --> 00:37:26,360
And in the US, there's a growing micro distillery movement

707
00:37:26,460 --> 00:37:28,060
that's not afraid to innovate.

708
00:37:28,160 --> 00:37:31,060
But what exactly does a gas pump have to do with whiskey?

709
00:37:35,460 --> 00:37:38,160
So what does it take to start your own micro distillery

710
00:37:38,260 --> 00:37:40,340
and make your own whiskey?

711
00:37:40,440 --> 00:37:43,480
For the team at Stranahan's Whiskey in Denver, Colorado,

712
00:37:43,580 --> 00:37:47,520
it took some cash, half a million dollars in seed money,

713
00:37:47,620 --> 00:37:49,880
and also a strong desire to work with their hands.

714
00:37:49,980 --> 00:37:53,120
Here at Stranahan's, we do everything by hand.

715
00:37:53,121 --> 00:37:54,920
so we actually handwrite the labels.

716
00:37:55,020 --> 00:37:57,920
We hand bottle everything.

717
00:37:58,020 --> 00:37:59,620
And it helps to have friends.

718
00:37:59,720 --> 00:38:03,360
Tomorrow we'll do about 25 to 3,000 bottles.

719
00:38:03,460 --> 00:38:05,960
We have volunteers come in and help us.

720
00:38:06,060 --> 00:38:08,460
And yes, you have to like whiskey.

721
00:38:08,560 --> 00:38:12,480
So, buddy.

722
00:38:12,580 --> 00:38:14,180
Man, that's good.

723
00:38:14,280 --> 00:38:16,720
Stranahan's Colorado whiskey is at the vanguard

724
00:38:16,820 --> 00:38:19,980
of a micro-distilling movement that over the last 20 years

725
00:38:20,080 --> 00:38:22,080
has seen scores of new distilleries opening

726
00:38:22,081 --> 00:38:25,420
across the United States.

727
00:38:25,520 --> 00:38:27,480
In part, this is due to relaxed regulation

728
00:38:27,580 --> 00:38:31,320
on alcohol production at the state level.

729
00:38:31,420 --> 00:38:33,660
States are increasingly turning to distilled spirits

730
00:38:33,760 --> 00:38:37,520
to generate much needed tax revenue.

731
00:38:37,620 --> 00:38:40,000
But it's not legal to distill at home,

732
00:38:40,100 --> 00:38:42,300
mostly because alcohol is highly flammable

733
00:38:42,400 --> 00:38:43,740
and presents a fire hazard.

734
00:38:43,840 --> 00:38:48,080
Well, micro distilling is growing very rapidly right now,

735
00:38:48,180 --> 00:38:51,380
kind of following the same path that the microbrew beer has

736
00:38:51,381 --> 00:38:57,220
It's a rebellion against the homogenization of America.

737
00:38:57,320 --> 00:38:59,260
So in the midst of so much sameness

738
00:38:59,360 --> 00:39:01,760
comes something a little out of the norm,

739
00:39:01,860 --> 00:39:04,020
a Rocky Mountain whiskey.

740
00:39:04,120 --> 00:39:06,400
We're the first straight American whiskey

741
00:39:06,500 --> 00:39:10,660
to be made of 100% Baltic Barley.

742
00:39:10,760 --> 00:39:13,140
Mashing, pasteurizing, and fermenting

743
00:39:13,240 --> 00:39:15,240
all take place next door to Stranahan's

744
00:39:15,340 --> 00:39:16,980
at the Flying Dog Brewery.

745
00:39:17,080 --> 00:39:19,500
So you use a lot of the same type of equipment

746
00:39:19,501 --> 00:39:21,540
equipment and a lot of the same gear.

747
00:39:21,640 --> 00:39:25,640
So it was a good fit to hire a established microbrewery

748
00:39:25,740 --> 00:39:27,000
to ferment our recipe.

749
00:39:27,100 --> 00:39:28,740
Otherwise, we'd have to purchase all this equipment

750
00:39:28,840 --> 00:39:30,640
ourselves.

751
00:39:30,740 --> 00:39:33,020
It only takes one of the brewery's 40 fermenting

752
00:39:33,120 --> 00:39:36,080
tanks to satisfy Stranahan's needs.

753
00:39:36,180 --> 00:39:37,280
Here we are.

754
00:39:37,380 --> 00:39:40,260
We're hanging on for dear life on fermenting vessel number four.

755
00:39:40,360 --> 00:39:42,520
This is where we're going to add the yeast,

756
00:39:42,620 --> 00:39:45,200
and the magic will happen.

757
00:39:45,300 --> 00:39:47,600
Each batch is about 3,000 gallons,

758
00:39:47,601 --> 00:39:50,500
And so that's what we'll distill over a week's time.

759
00:39:50,600 --> 00:39:53,180
Fline Dog ferments our recipe to our specs

760
00:39:53,280 --> 00:39:56,340
and then pipes it over the roof.

761
00:39:56,440 --> 00:39:59,580
The freshly fermented beer, also known as wash,

762
00:39:59,680 --> 00:40:03,620
is ready to be distilled in Stranahan's custom-designed still.

763
00:40:03,720 --> 00:40:05,360
They're unique in design in the fact

764
00:40:05,460 --> 00:40:08,220
that the bottom part of the still is a pot still, all right?

765
00:40:08,320 --> 00:40:09,420
And that's like scotch.

766
00:40:09,520 --> 00:40:12,320
Scotch is generally made in a pot still in a batch.

767
00:40:12,420 --> 00:40:16,000
The upper part of the still is a column still.

768
00:40:16,001 --> 00:40:18,640
We're using kind of bourbon technology up on top

769
00:40:18,740 --> 00:40:20,740
and Scott's technology in the bottom.

770
00:40:20,840 --> 00:40:23,100
We were the first people to use this.

771
00:40:23,200 --> 00:40:26,640
What we're trying to do is create a spirit that kind of

772
00:40:26,740 --> 00:40:32,040
takes on features of both types of distillate.

773
00:40:32,140 --> 00:40:34,760
Each batch of whiskey takes 18 hours to distill.

774
00:40:34,860 --> 00:40:36,920
That's why it's known as a batch process,

775
00:40:37,020 --> 00:40:39,760
because we're going to fill up our washed still

776
00:40:39,860 --> 00:40:42,360
with 750 gallons of wash.

777
00:40:42,460 --> 00:40:46,000
Once we distill out all the alcohol that's in there,

778
00:40:46,001 --> 00:40:49,100
we're going to drain it and refill it again.

779
00:40:49,200 --> 00:40:51,300
The barrel filler is, ingeniously enough,

780
00:40:51,400 --> 00:40:53,440
a modified gas pump.

781
00:40:53,540 --> 00:40:57,980
We're going to take our handy-dandy barrel filler,

782
00:40:58,080 --> 00:41:00,840
specific only to Stranahan's Colorado whiskey,

783
00:41:00,940 --> 00:41:04,320
and we're going to fill our new American White Oak

784
00:41:04,420 --> 00:41:05,480
heavy charred barrel.

785
00:41:05,580 --> 00:41:08,360
We fill this up.

786
00:41:08,460 --> 00:41:12,260
We're going to roll it back into our warehouse.

787
00:41:12,360 --> 00:41:15,060
Our rack house is unique in that we

788
00:41:15,061 --> 00:41:18,300
We are a heated and humidified facility here.

789
00:41:18,301 --> 00:41:19,900
In the wintertime, we're hot in here.

790
00:41:19,901 --> 00:41:21,900
And in the summertime, it's Hades in here.

791
00:41:21,901 --> 00:41:23,800
It gets to be about 110.

792
00:41:23,801 --> 00:41:26,540
So we have the two seasons to help force the whiskey

793
00:41:26,541 --> 00:41:27,800
in and out of the wood.

794
00:41:27,801 --> 00:41:31,420
So actually, our aging process is a little more rapid

795
00:41:31,421 --> 00:41:33,820
than most other distillers use.

796
00:41:33,821 --> 00:41:38,320
Right now, we're producing an average of six barrels a week.

797
00:41:38,321 --> 00:41:45,320
That's roughly 720 bottles of whiskey a week.

798
00:41:45,420 --> 00:41:47,360
But is the whiskey world better off

799
00:41:47,460 --> 00:41:49,160
for having a Colorado whiskey?

800
00:41:49,260 --> 00:41:51,860
We went back to the Brandy Library in Lower Manhattan

801
00:41:51,960 --> 00:41:53,260
to find out.

802
00:41:53,360 --> 00:41:55,140
I'm a little excited about this one,

803
00:41:55,240 --> 00:41:58,040
because I have yet to even come across it.

804
00:41:58,140 --> 00:41:59,500
It's almost orange.

805
00:41:59,600 --> 00:42:01,440
Eye catching right off the start.

806
00:42:04,480 --> 00:42:07,120
And the fruit pops.

807
00:42:07,121 --> 00:42:07,821
pops.

808
00:42:11,720 --> 00:42:13,920
And wow, that's impressive.

809
00:42:14,020 --> 00:42:15,520
That is very impressive.

810
00:42:15,620 --> 00:42:17,220
There's something cinnamon about it.

811
00:42:17,320 --> 00:42:19,260
It's spicy and fresh.

812
00:42:19,360 --> 00:42:20,460
That is a lovely dram.

813
00:42:20,560 --> 00:42:22,660
That is a perfect example of what

814
00:42:22,760 --> 00:42:27,980
can be done with just a little bit of ingenuity.

815
00:42:28,080 --> 00:42:30,080
And after all, it seems fitting that Colorado

816
00:42:30,180 --> 00:42:32,480
would produce a whiskey.

817
00:42:32,580 --> 00:42:35,120
We didn't have John Wayne walk into a bar in Colorado

818
00:42:35,121 --> 00:42:37,760
and say, hey, Pard, how about a mango-infused vodka

819
00:42:37,761 --> 00:42:38,720
and make it a double?

820
00:42:40,100 --> 00:42:41,900
He went in and he asked for a whiskey.

821
00:42:42,020 --> 00:42:44,020
That's what the miners and the cowboys drank.

822
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:45,700
Cheers.

823
00:42:45,840 --> 00:42:46,540
Good to see you.

824
00:42:46,900 --> 00:42:48,840
Whiskey is, without a doubt,

825
00:42:49,280 --> 00:42:52,000
the most complex and diverse spirit

826
00:42:52,001 --> 00:42:54,000
being produced on the face of this earth.

827
00:42:55,160 --> 00:42:56,540
And as whiskey producers

828
00:42:56,541 --> 00:42:58,680
make an ever-increasing variety of whiskeys,

829
00:42:59,160 --> 00:43:01,260
there are more and more good times to drink them.

830
00:43:01,860 --> 00:43:03,180
People get this misconception

831
00:43:03,181 --> 00:43:05,320
that whiskey is an after dinner drink,

832
00:43:05,420 --> 00:43:07,520
and that's the only time you should drink it.

833
00:43:07,620 --> 00:43:10,520
What they don't realize is that there's some great whiskeys

834
00:43:10,620 --> 00:43:12,420
that go with a meal.

835
00:43:12,520 --> 00:43:15,320
There's some great aperitif whiskeys.

836
00:43:15,420 --> 00:43:16,920
There's some great afternoon whiskeys,

837
00:43:17,020 --> 00:43:20,340
and there's some great mid to late morning whiskeys, too.

838
00:43:20,440 --> 00:43:24,340
Now there's a concept, whiskey with your coffee break.

839
00:43:24,440 --> 00:43:27,280
If you drink enough, you might not get much done,

840
00:43:27,380 --> 00:43:28,680
but you'll think you have.

